1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,652
Imagine you're holding a single dollar bill

2
00:00:02,652 --> 00:00:04,133
in nineteen fifty-eight.

3
00:00:04,313 --> 00:00:06,739
A crisp green George Washington.

4
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:08,637
It doesn't sound like much.

5
00:00:08,817 --> 00:00:10,476
But in nineteen fifty-eight,

6
00:00:10,476 --> 00:00:12,905
that dollar could take you a surprisingly

7
00:00:12,905 --> 00:00:13,438
long way.

8
00:00:13,618 --> 00:00:15,487
It could buy you lunch,

9
00:00:15,487 --> 00:00:17,600
a movie, a tank of gas for

10
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:20,201
the lawnmower, or a whole bag of

11
00:00:20,201 --> 00:00:21,014
groceries.

12
00:00:21,194 --> 00:00:23,551
So what could a dollar really buy?

13
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,025
First, some perspective.

14
00:00:26,205 --> 00:00:27,592
In nineteen fifty-eight,

15
00:00:27,592 --> 00:00:30,193
the average American family earned about five

16
00:00:30,193 --> 00:00:31,581
thousand dollars a year.

17
00:00:31,761 --> 00:00:34,080
The minimum wage was a dollar an

18
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:34,442
hour.

19
00:00:34,622 --> 00:00:36,866
So a dollar was about an hour

20
00:00:36,866 --> 00:00:39,031
of work for a teenager or an

21
00:00:39,031 --> 00:00:40,346
unskilled worker.

22
00:00:40,526 --> 00:00:42,259
And because of inflation,

23
00:00:42,259 --> 00:00:45,239
one dollar in nineteen fifty-eight is worth

24
00:00:45,239 --> 00:00:47,248
roughly eleven dollars today.

25
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:49,908
So let's take that dollar and spend

26
00:00:49,908 --> 00:00:50,106
it.

27
00:00:50,286 --> 00:00:51,714
All over town.

28
00:00:51,894 --> 00:00:53,606
At the grocery store,

29
00:00:53,606 --> 00:00:55,807
the diner, the gas station,

30
00:00:55,807 --> 00:00:57,275
the movie theater,

31
00:00:57,275 --> 00:00:59,476
the five-and-dime and more.

32
00:00:59,656 --> 00:01:00,741
And at each stop,

33
00:01:00,741 --> 00:01:02,401
we'll see how far it went,

34
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:04,509
and how that compares with today.

35
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:06,889
Some of these prices will make you

36
00:01:06,889 --> 00:01:07,278
smile.

37
00:01:07,627 --> 00:01:10,066
And money itself looked and felt different.

38
00:01:10,246 --> 00:01:11,863
In nineteen fifty-eight,

39
00:01:11,863 --> 00:01:14,760
dimes, quarters and half dollars were still

40
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:16,782
made of ninety percent silver.

41
00:01:16,962 --> 00:01:19,943
A silver dollar coin was still common

42
00:01:19,943 --> 00:01:20,909
in the West.

43
00:01:21,089 --> 00:01:22,958
People carried more cash,

44
00:01:22,958 --> 00:01:25,873
and paid for almost everything with it.

45
00:01:26,053 --> 00:01:27,478
Checks were for bills,

46
00:01:27,478 --> 00:01:29,617
and credit cards were still rare.

47
00:01:29,797 --> 00:01:32,197
A man's wallet and a woman's coin

48
00:01:32,197 --> 00:01:35,033
purse held the family's spending money.

49
00:01:35,382 --> 00:01:37,565
Let's start at the grocery store.

50
00:01:37,745 --> 00:01:39,452
In nineteen fifty-eight,

51
00:01:39,452 --> 00:01:41,443
a dollar could buy a loaf of

52
00:01:41,443 --> 00:01:44,073
white bread for about nineteen cents,

53
00:01:44,073 --> 00:01:46,704
a quart of milk for about twenty-five

54
00:01:46,704 --> 00:01:49,193
cents, a dozen eggs for about sixty

55
00:01:49,193 --> 00:01:51,753
cents, and you'd still have a little

56
00:01:51,753 --> 00:01:52,606
change left.

57
00:01:52,786 --> 00:01:54,774
Or a pound of hamburger and a

58
00:01:54,774 --> 00:01:56,008
pound of potatoes,

59
00:01:56,008 --> 00:01:57,790
with a few cents to spare.

60
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,750
A pound of coffee cost about ninety

61
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:03,062
cents, almost the whole dollar.

62
00:02:03,242 --> 00:02:05,413
That was considered expensive,

63
00:02:05,413 --> 00:02:08,525
and families watched coffee prices closely.

64
00:02:08,705 --> 00:02:11,351
A can of Campbell's soup was about

65
00:02:11,351 --> 00:02:12,130
ten cents.

66
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:14,273
A box of corn flakes,

67
00:02:14,273 --> 00:02:15,955
about twenty-five.

68
00:02:16,135 --> 00:02:17,631
A pound of butter,

69
00:02:17,631 --> 00:02:19,792
around seventy-five cents.

70
00:02:19,972 --> 00:02:22,545
Many families bought margarine instead,

71
00:02:22,545 --> 00:02:24,523
because it was half the price.

72
00:02:24,874 --> 00:02:27,648
Fresh fruit and vegetables were cheap in

73
00:02:27,648 --> 00:02:30,144
season, and expensive out of season.

74
00:02:30,324 --> 00:02:32,801
Bananas might be about fifteen cents a

75
00:02:32,801 --> 00:02:33,192
pound.

76
00:02:33,372 --> 00:02:34,990
A head of lettuce,

77
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:36,159
twenty cents.

78
00:02:36,339 --> 00:02:39,134
But strawberries in January were rare and

79
00:02:39,134 --> 00:02:42,270
costly, because there was little shipping from

80
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:43,293
faraway places.

81
00:02:43,473 --> 00:02:45,909
Families ate what was in season,

82
00:02:45,909 --> 00:02:48,117
and canned or froze the rest.

83
00:02:48,467 --> 00:02:51,418
Shopping was also a game of stamps

84
00:02:51,418 --> 00:02:52,460
and coupons.

85
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:55,429
Many supermarkets gave out S and H

86
00:02:55,429 --> 00:02:58,464
Green Stamps or Top Value Stamps with

87
00:02:58,464 --> 00:03:01,827
every purchase, about one stamp for every

88
00:03:01,827 --> 00:03:02,729
dime spent.

89
00:03:02,909 --> 00:03:04,426
Families licked them,

90
00:03:04,426 --> 00:03:06,592
pasted them into little books,

91
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:09,119
and traded full books for toasters,

92
00:03:09,119 --> 00:03:12,151
lamps and towels at the redemption center.

93
00:03:12,331 --> 00:03:15,036
Many kitchens had something bought with stamps.

94
00:03:15,386 --> 00:03:16,930
So how does that compare?

95
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:19,871
Today, that dollar's worth of groceries,

96
00:03:19,871 --> 00:03:20,975
a loaf of bread,

97
00:03:20,975 --> 00:03:22,839
a quart of milk and a dozen

98
00:03:22,839 --> 00:03:25,599
eggs, would cost something like eight to

99
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:28,429
ten dollars, depending on where you live.

100
00:03:28,609 --> 00:03:31,110
Food prices have gone up roughly the

101
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:32,777
same as everything else.

102
00:03:32,957 --> 00:03:35,638
But families today spend a smaller share

103
00:03:35,638 --> 00:03:37,850
of their income on food than they

104
00:03:37,850 --> 00:03:39,726
did in nineteen fifty-eight.

105
00:03:40,076 --> 00:03:41,643
Now let's go to lunch.

106
00:03:41,823 --> 00:03:43,966
At a diner or lunch counter in

107
00:03:43,966 --> 00:03:47,109
nineteen fifty-eight, a dollar could buy you

108
00:03:47,109 --> 00:03:48,966
a hamburger, French fries,

109
00:03:48,966 --> 00:03:50,681
a slice of pie and a cup

110
00:03:50,681 --> 00:03:53,110
of coffee, with a nickel left over

111
00:03:53,110 --> 00:03:53,967
for the tip.

112
00:03:54,147 --> 00:03:57,457
A hamburger might cost thirty to thirty-five

113
00:03:57,457 --> 00:03:57,909
cents.

114
00:03:58,089 --> 00:03:59,353
Coffee was a dime,

115
00:03:59,353 --> 00:04:00,898
and refills were free.

116
00:04:01,249 --> 00:04:03,299
At the new fast food chains,

117
00:04:03,299 --> 00:04:04,836
it went even further.

118
00:04:05,016 --> 00:04:06,654
In nineteen fifty-eight,

119
00:04:06,654 --> 00:04:09,521
a McDonald's hamburger cost fifteen cents.

120
00:04:09,701 --> 00:04:11,683
French fries were ten cents,

121
00:04:11,683 --> 00:04:13,311
and a shake was twenty.

122
00:04:13,491 --> 00:04:15,534
So a dollar could buy lunch for

123
00:04:15,534 --> 00:04:17,182
two people at McDonald's,

124
00:04:17,182 --> 00:04:17,973
with change.

125
00:04:18,153 --> 00:04:21,517
McDonald's signs proudly counted how many millions

126
00:04:21,517 --> 00:04:23,401
of hamburgers they had sold.

127
00:04:23,751 --> 00:04:25,211
At a soda fountain,

128
00:04:25,211 --> 00:04:26,978
a dollar was a fortune.

129
00:04:27,158 --> 00:04:28,515
A Coke was a nickel,

130
00:04:28,515 --> 00:04:30,687
and had been for more than fifty

131
00:04:30,687 --> 00:04:31,094
years.

132
00:04:31,274 --> 00:04:33,988
An ice cream soda was about twenty

133
00:04:33,988 --> 00:04:34,467
cents.

134
00:04:34,647 --> 00:04:36,516
A milkshake, a quarter.

135
00:04:36,696 --> 00:04:37,701
With a dollar,

136
00:04:37,701 --> 00:04:40,286
a teenager could treat three or four

137
00:04:40,286 --> 00:04:42,512
friends to a round of Cokes and

138
00:04:42,512 --> 00:04:44,881
still have money for the jukebox.

139
00:04:45,231 --> 00:04:46,999
And at a nicer restaurant,

140
00:04:46,999 --> 00:04:49,108
a dollar still went a long way.

141
00:04:49,288 --> 00:04:50,750
A complete dinner,

142
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:52,130
with soup, salad,

143
00:04:52,130 --> 00:04:54,972
a main course like roast chicken or

144
00:04:54,972 --> 00:04:56,109
a small steak,

145
00:04:56,109 --> 00:04:58,545
vegetables, rolls and dessert,

146
00:04:58,545 --> 00:05:00,981
might cost a dollar and a half

147
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:02,199
to two dollars.

148
00:05:02,379 --> 00:05:04,764
Families went out to eat much less

149
00:05:04,764 --> 00:05:05,957
often than today,

150
00:05:05,957 --> 00:05:08,483
so a restaurant dinner was a special

151
00:05:08,483 --> 00:05:11,150
occasion, usually for a birthday or an

152
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:11,992
anniversary.

153
00:05:12,342 --> 00:05:14,083
Now let's fill up the car.

154
00:05:14,263 --> 00:05:16,163
In nineteen fifty-eight,

155
00:05:16,163 --> 00:05:19,249
a gallon of regular gasoline cost about

156
00:05:19,249 --> 00:05:21,624
thirty cents, including taxes.

157
00:05:21,804 --> 00:05:24,313
So a dollar bought more than three

158
00:05:24,313 --> 00:05:24,904
gallons.

159
00:05:25,084 --> 00:05:26,961
In a big car that got about

160
00:05:26,961 --> 00:05:28,908
fifteen miles to the gallon,

161
00:05:28,908 --> 00:05:31,064
that was a drive of about fifty

162
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:31,481
miles.

163
00:05:31,661 --> 00:05:33,791
Enough for a Sunday drive in the

164
00:05:33,791 --> 00:05:34,923
country and back.

165
00:05:35,274 --> 00:05:36,585
And with that dollar,

166
00:05:36,585 --> 00:05:38,083
you didn't just get gas.

167
00:05:38,263 --> 00:05:40,809
An attendant in a uniform pumped it

168
00:05:40,809 --> 00:05:43,136
for you, washed your windshield,

169
00:05:43,136 --> 00:05:45,682
checked your oil and tire pressure,

170
00:05:45,682 --> 00:05:47,863
and might even give you a free

171
00:05:47,863 --> 00:05:49,754
glass, a road map or a set

172
00:05:49,754 --> 00:05:50,918
of steak knives.

173
00:05:51,098 --> 00:05:53,257
Full service was just how it was

174
00:05:53,257 --> 00:05:53,594
done.

175
00:05:53,945 --> 00:05:54,684
A new car,

176
00:05:54,684 --> 00:05:55,498
by the way,

177
00:05:55,498 --> 00:05:58,752
cost about two thousand five hundred dollars

178
00:05:58,752 --> 00:06:00,526
in nineteen fifty-eight,

179
00:06:00,526 --> 00:06:02,745
for a basic Chevrolet or Ford.

180
00:06:02,925 --> 00:06:05,042
That was about half a year's income

181
00:06:05,042 --> 00:06:06,373
for an average family.

182
00:06:06,553 --> 00:06:09,046
Today, a new car costs well over

183
00:06:09,046 --> 00:06:11,695
forty thousand dollars on average,

184
00:06:11,695 --> 00:06:14,343
closer to most of a year's income.

185
00:06:14,694 --> 00:06:17,012
Even keeping up a car was cheap

186
00:06:17,012 --> 00:06:18,583
by today's standards.

187
00:06:18,763 --> 00:06:21,149
An oil change might cost a couple

188
00:06:21,149 --> 00:06:21,944
of dollars.

189
00:06:22,124 --> 00:06:22,926
A new tire,

190
00:06:22,926 --> 00:06:25,259
about fifteen to twenty dollars.

191
00:06:25,439 --> 00:06:27,987
And many men did their own repairs,

192
00:06:27,987 --> 00:06:30,026
with a few basic tools and a

193
00:06:30,026 --> 00:06:30,536
manual.

194
00:06:30,716 --> 00:06:33,026
But cars wore out faster too.

195
00:06:33,206 --> 00:06:35,875
By sixty or seventy thousand miles,

196
00:06:35,875 --> 00:06:37,629
many needed major work.

197
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:39,477
Next, the movies.

198
00:06:39,657 --> 00:06:41,301
In nineteen fifty-eight,

199
00:06:41,301 --> 00:06:43,904
an adult movie ticket cost about fifty

200
00:06:43,904 --> 00:06:46,575
to seventy-five cents in most theaters,

201
00:06:46,575 --> 00:06:48,492
and children paid a quarter.

202
00:06:48,672 --> 00:06:50,648
So a dollar could take a child

203
00:06:50,648 --> 00:06:52,295
to the movies four times.

204
00:06:52,475 --> 00:06:55,030
Or it could buy an adult ticket,

205
00:06:55,030 --> 00:06:57,265
a box of popcorn for a dime,

206
00:06:57,265 --> 00:06:59,580
and a candy bar for a nickel.

207
00:06:59,930 --> 00:07:02,841
The Saturday matinee was the best deal

208
00:07:02,841 --> 00:07:03,378
of all.

209
00:07:03,558 --> 00:07:04,721
For a quarter,

210
00:07:04,721 --> 00:07:06,632
a child got a newsreel,

211
00:07:06,632 --> 00:07:08,793
cartoons, a cowboy serial,

212
00:07:08,793 --> 00:07:10,953
and a full-length feature.

213
00:07:11,133 --> 00:07:13,618
Some theaters ran double features.

214
00:07:13,798 --> 00:07:16,239
Parents dropped the kids off at noon

215
00:07:16,239 --> 00:07:18,070
and picked them up at four.

216
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:20,274
That was a lot of entertainment for

217
00:07:20,274 --> 00:07:21,315
very little money.

218
00:07:21,666 --> 00:07:22,795
At the drive-in,

219
00:07:22,795 --> 00:07:24,278
it went even further.

220
00:07:24,458 --> 00:07:27,202
Many drive-ins charged by the carload,

221
00:07:27,202 --> 00:07:29,296
a dollar for the whole car on

222
00:07:29,296 --> 00:07:30,379
bargain nights.

223
00:07:30,559 --> 00:07:32,538
A family of six could see two

224
00:07:32,538 --> 00:07:33,903
movies for a dollar.

225
00:07:34,083 --> 00:07:37,014
That's one reason drive-ins were so popular

226
00:07:37,014 --> 00:07:39,400
with young families in the fifties.

227
00:07:39,750 --> 00:07:41,747
Now let's go to the five-and-dime.

228
00:07:41,927 --> 00:07:44,806
Woolworth's, Kresge's or Ben Franklin.

229
00:07:44,986 --> 00:07:46,755
In nineteen fifty-eight,

230
00:07:46,755 --> 00:07:49,409
the name was a little old-fashioned,

231
00:07:49,409 --> 00:07:51,841
but many things still cost a dime

232
00:07:51,841 --> 00:07:52,799
or a quarter.

233
00:07:52,979 --> 00:07:55,275
A dollar could buy a spool of

234
00:07:55,275 --> 00:07:57,333
thread, a pack of needles,

235
00:07:57,333 --> 00:07:59,311
a comb, a box of crayons,

236
00:07:59,311 --> 00:08:00,578
a coloring book,

237
00:08:00,578 --> 00:08:02,082
and a bag of candy.

238
00:08:02,432 --> 00:08:05,004
For kids, the five-and-dime was where a

239
00:08:05,004 --> 00:08:06,785
dollar felt like a million.

240
00:08:06,965 --> 00:08:09,276
Comic books cost a dime each.

241
00:08:09,456 --> 00:08:12,163
Baseball cards were a penny or a

242
00:08:12,163 --> 00:08:13,347
nickel a pack,

243
00:08:13,347 --> 00:08:15,969
with a slab of pink bubble gum.

244
00:08:16,149 --> 00:08:17,948
A yo-yo was about a quarter.

245
00:08:18,128 --> 00:08:19,977
A balsa wood glider,

246
00:08:19,977 --> 00:08:20,625
a dime.

247
00:08:20,805 --> 00:08:22,664
A bag of marbles,

248
00:08:22,664 --> 00:08:24,195
fifteen cents.

249
00:08:24,375 --> 00:08:26,591
A child with a dollar could walk

250
00:08:26,591 --> 00:08:28,252
out with a whole armful.

251
00:08:28,603 --> 00:08:31,226
Penny candy was really a penny.

252
00:08:31,406 --> 00:08:33,602
Tootsie Rolls, Mary Janes,

253
00:08:33,602 --> 00:08:35,967
Bazooka gum, licorice whips,

254
00:08:35,967 --> 00:08:39,092
candy buttons on paper strips and wax

255
00:08:39,092 --> 00:08:41,879
bottles with sweet colored syrup.

256
00:08:42,059 --> 00:08:44,195
A candy bar was a nickel.

257
00:08:44,375 --> 00:08:46,161
A kid with a dime could get

258
00:08:46,161 --> 00:08:48,277
a little paper bag full of candy

259
00:08:48,277 --> 00:08:50,261
that would last all afternoon.

260
00:08:50,611 --> 00:08:51,714
And there were records.

261
00:08:51,894 --> 00:08:54,334
A forty-five r p m single,

262
00:08:54,334 --> 00:08:56,869
with one song on each side,

263
00:08:56,869 --> 00:08:59,591
cost about eighty-nine cents.

264
00:08:59,771 --> 00:09:01,037
So with a dollar,

265
00:09:01,037 --> 00:09:03,644
a teenager could buy the latest hit

266
00:09:03,644 --> 00:09:06,475
by Elvis, the Everly Brothers or Ricky

267
00:09:06,475 --> 00:09:09,082
Nelson, and still have a dime left.

268
00:09:09,262 --> 00:09:12,417
A long-playing album cost about three or

269
00:09:12,417 --> 00:09:16,124
four dollars, so most teenagers bought singles.

270
00:09:16,474 --> 00:09:18,308
Let's keep going around town.

271
00:09:18,488 --> 00:09:21,086
A haircut at the barbershop cost about

272
00:09:21,086 --> 00:09:22,864
a dollar to a dollar and a

273
00:09:22,864 --> 00:09:24,026
quarter, for men.

274
00:09:24,206 --> 00:09:27,108
For boys, maybe seventy-five cents.

275
00:09:27,288 --> 00:09:29,309
A shoeshine was a quarter.

276
00:09:29,489 --> 00:09:32,439
A daily newspaper was usually a nickel

277
00:09:32,439 --> 00:09:33,215
or a dime.

278
00:09:33,395 --> 00:09:36,311
A first-class stamp was four cents,

279
00:09:36,311 --> 00:09:39,393
after August of nineteen fifty-eight,

280
00:09:39,393 --> 00:09:40,559
up from three.

281
00:09:40,909 --> 00:09:43,087
A phone call from a pay phone

282
00:09:43,087 --> 00:09:45,115
cost a dime in many places,

283
00:09:45,115 --> 00:09:48,269
though some cities still charged a nickel.

284
00:09:48,449 --> 00:09:51,062
A city bus ride was about fifteen

285
00:09:51,062 --> 00:09:51,538
cents.

286
00:09:51,718 --> 00:09:55,212
A pack of cigarettes cost around twenty-five

287
00:09:55,212 --> 00:09:55,688
cents.

288
00:09:55,868 --> 00:09:57,638
And a bottle of aspirin,

289
00:09:57,638 --> 00:09:59,409
or a tube of toothpaste,

290
00:09:59,409 --> 00:10:00,884
cost about the same.

291
00:10:01,234 --> 00:10:03,322
A visit to the family doctor might

292
00:10:03,322 --> 00:10:04,980
cost three to five dollars,

293
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,946
and he might come to your house.

294
00:10:07,126 --> 00:10:09,550
A night in a roadside motel,

295
00:10:09,550 --> 00:10:12,060
around five to seven dollars.

296
00:10:12,240 --> 00:10:14,695
A gallon of milk delivered to the

297
00:10:14,695 --> 00:10:16,257
door, about a dollar.

298
00:10:16,437 --> 00:10:18,625
And a pair of Levi's jeans cost

299
00:10:18,625 --> 00:10:19,966
about four dollars.

300
00:10:20,317 --> 00:10:22,277
And money could even be made from

301
00:10:22,277 --> 00:10:22,871
the trash.

302
00:10:23,051 --> 00:10:25,677
Soda bottles carried a deposit of two

303
00:10:25,677 --> 00:10:28,517
cents each, and kids collected them from

304
00:10:28,517 --> 00:10:31,215
ditches and empty lots and turned them

305
00:10:31,215 --> 00:10:32,350
in at the store.

306
00:10:32,530 --> 00:10:35,474
Newspapers and scrap metal could be sold

307
00:10:35,474 --> 00:10:36,652
to the junk man.

308
00:10:36,832 --> 00:10:39,137
A sharp-eyed kid with a wagon could

309
00:10:39,137 --> 00:10:40,982
earn a dollar on a Saturday,

310
00:10:40,982 --> 00:10:43,090
and spend it the same afternoon.

311
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:45,355
What about the big things?

312
00:10:45,535 --> 00:10:48,481
The average new house in nineteen fifty-eight

313
00:10:48,481 --> 00:10:50,771
cost about twelve thousand dollars.

314
00:10:50,951 --> 00:10:53,389
A refrigerator, about three hundred.

315
00:10:53,569 --> 00:10:55,354
A new television set,

316
00:10:55,354 --> 00:10:56,714
black and white,

317
00:10:56,714 --> 00:10:58,330
around two hundred.

318
00:10:58,510 --> 00:10:59,986
A washing machine,

319
00:10:59,986 --> 00:11:02,283
about two hundred and fifty.

320
00:11:02,463 --> 00:11:04,380
Those were major purchases,

321
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:06,793
often bought on installment plans.

322
00:11:07,144 --> 00:11:08,084
And college?

323
00:11:08,264 --> 00:11:11,198
At a public university in nineteen fifty-eight,

324
00:11:11,198 --> 00:11:13,446
tuition might be a couple of hundred

325
00:11:13,446 --> 00:11:14,382
dollars a year.

326
00:11:14,562 --> 00:11:15,664
Room and board,

327
00:11:15,664 --> 00:11:17,059
a few hundred more.

328
00:11:17,239 --> 00:11:19,283
A student could earn most of a

329
00:11:19,283 --> 00:11:21,941
year's college costs with a summer job.

330
00:11:22,121 --> 00:11:24,461
Today, the cost of a year at

331
00:11:24,461 --> 00:11:27,888
a public university is many times higher,

332
00:11:27,888 --> 00:11:29,644
even after inflation.

333
00:11:29,994 --> 00:11:31,463
Some things, though,

334
00:11:31,463 --> 00:11:34,991
were more expensive in nineteen fifty-eight than

335
00:11:34,991 --> 00:11:37,122
today, compared with incomes.

336
00:11:37,302 --> 00:11:40,576
Long distance phone calls cost a fortune.

337
00:11:40,756 --> 00:11:42,408
Air travel was a luxury,

338
00:11:42,408 --> 00:11:45,092
and a cross-country flight could cost a

339
00:11:45,092 --> 00:11:46,538
couple of weeks' pay.

340
00:11:46,718 --> 00:11:50,255
Televisions, radios and other electronics took a

341
00:11:50,255 --> 00:11:52,244
big bite out of a paycheck.

342
00:11:52,424 --> 00:11:54,825
Today, many of those things are far

343
00:11:54,825 --> 00:11:55,373
cheaper.

344
00:11:55,724 --> 00:11:58,673
So was life cheaper in nineteen fifty-eight?

345
00:11:58,853 --> 00:12:00,326
In some ways,

346
00:12:00,326 --> 00:12:00,780
yes.

347
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:03,941
Housing, college, cars and food took a

348
00:12:03,941 --> 00:12:07,001
smaller share of a family's income than

349
00:12:07,001 --> 00:12:08,100
they do today.

350
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:10,985
A single paycheck could support a family.

351
00:12:11,165 --> 00:12:12,762
In other ways,

352
00:12:12,762 --> 00:12:13,104
no.

353
00:12:13,284 --> 00:12:14,917
People earned much less,

354
00:12:14,917 --> 00:12:17,026
and many things we now take for

355
00:12:17,026 --> 00:12:18,523
granted were luxuries,

356
00:12:18,523 --> 00:12:20,087
or didn't exist at all.

357
00:12:20,438 --> 00:12:22,502
But maybe the real difference was how

358
00:12:22,502 --> 00:12:24,176
people thought about a dollar.

359
00:12:24,356 --> 00:12:25,822
In nineteen fifty-eight,

360
00:12:25,822 --> 00:12:28,570
many adults had lived through the Depression.

361
00:12:28,750 --> 00:12:30,107
They knew what it meant to have

362
00:12:30,107 --> 00:12:30,457
nothing.

363
00:12:30,637 --> 00:12:32,599
So they respected every dollar.

364
00:12:32,779 --> 00:12:34,330
They saved, they budgeted,

365
00:12:34,330 --> 00:12:36,775
and they taught their children that money

366
00:12:36,775 --> 00:12:38,027
didn't grow on trees.

367
00:12:38,377 --> 00:12:40,503
If you were a child in nineteen

368
00:12:40,503 --> 00:12:44,139
fifty-eight, you might remember your first allowance,

369
00:12:44,139 --> 00:12:46,335
maybe a quarter or fifty cents a

370
00:12:46,335 --> 00:12:46,678
week.

371
00:12:46,858 --> 00:12:49,029
You remember exactly what it bought.

372
00:12:49,209 --> 00:12:50,521
A comic book,

373
00:12:50,521 --> 00:12:52,843
a candy bar and a Coke.

374
00:12:53,023 --> 00:12:55,292
And you remember the feeling of walking

375
00:12:55,292 --> 00:12:57,212
into a store with your own money,

376
00:12:57,212 --> 00:12:58,259
ready to spend it.

377
00:12:58,609 --> 00:13:00,397
So tell us in the comments.

378
00:13:00,577 --> 00:13:02,671
What was your allowance when you were

379
00:13:02,671 --> 00:13:04,312
a kid, and what did you spend

380
00:13:04,312 --> 00:13:04,652
it on?

381
00:13:04,832 --> 00:13:06,726
What price from the old days do

382
00:13:06,726 --> 00:13:08,071
you remember the most?

383
00:13:08,251 --> 00:13:09,702
We read every comment.

384
00:13:09,882 --> 00:13:12,778
Next time, we're remembering the men who

385
00:13:12,778 --> 00:13:15,457
came home on Veterans Day in nineteen

386
00:13:15,457 --> 00:13:17,556
fifty-five and built America.

387
00:13:17,736 --> 00:13:19,691
Thank you for spending this time with

388
00:13:19,691 --> 00:13:19,849
me.
